From e81c6580893dfd4e4254a9ac59747d27c7a3bc86 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tillmann Severin <tillmann.severin@mailbox.org>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 18:14:36 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 40/49] media: platform: bring up the HiSilicon set-top box
 demultiplexer input

The demultiplexer is switched off at boot: its reset is asserted and
none of its clocks run, so every register in the block reads back as
zero.  That is indistinguishable from an idle block, and it is why the
hardware appeared to be absent.

Start the clocks, release the reset, put the four pads that carry the
serial transport stream into their transport mode, and tell the input
port what to expect.  The block then counts the packets arriving on
each port, which is reported through debugfs.  A tuned front end shows
up there as a steady packet rate with no errors, so the input path can
be confirmed before any filtering exists.

Filtering and delivery to the DVB stack are not here yet.  The output
side is a descriptor ring rather than a simple buffer and deserves to
be a change of its own.

The transport clock source for input group 0 is selected in the clock
and reset controller, on a path the clock framework cannot describe,
so the driver reaches it through a syscon reference.

Co-authored-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
